                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>309. <hi rendition="#italic">Proposed Air Routes for Inter-Zonal
                    Flights</hi>.</p>
                <p>The meeting considered CORC/P(45)170.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">General Robertson</hi><anchor n="73"
                        xml:id="d1200-f2"/> explained that it was absolutely essential for the
                    British to have air corridors both for flights from Berlin to Bukeburg [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bückeburg</hi>], Headquarters of the British Army of the
                    Rhine, and from Berlin to Hamburg, where this headquarters had a number of
                    branches.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">General Clay</hi><anchor n="74" xml:id="d1200-f3"/>
                    also pointed out that the Berlin-Bukeburg [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bückeburg</hi>] air corridor was important for the Americans since it
                    enabled them to maintain their connection with Bremen.<anchor n="75"
                        xml:id="d1200-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">General Sokolovsky</hi><anchor n="76"
                        xml:id="d1200-f5"/> then stated that he was not empowered to settle
                    questions concerning other air corridors mentioned in the paper, viz.:
                    Berlin-Warsaw, Berlin-Prague, Berlin-Copenhagen and Bukeburg [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bückeburg</hi>]-Prague, as these required decisions at
                    governmental level.</p>
                <p>On <hi rendition="#small-caps">General Sokolovsky’s</hi> proposal</p>
                <p>The Meeting:</p>
                <p>(309) Approved CORC/P(45)170 and agreed to submit it to the Control Council for
                    confirmation of that portion which deals with air corridors from Berlin to the
                    West.</p>
